{"questions":[{"question_id":"rKpvjN3h9RhkMatE","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2003","year":2003,"examDate":2003,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"alcohols-phenols-and-ethers","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","topic":"properties-preparation-and-uses-of-ethers","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"an-ethar-is-more-volatile-than-an-alcohol-having-the-same-mo-2003-marks-4-rkpvjn3h9rhkmate.htm","content":"An ethar is more volatile than an alcohol having the same molecular formula. This is due to","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"alcohols having resonance structures"},{"identifier":"B","content":"inter-molecular hydrogen bonding in ethers"},{"identifier":"C","content":"inter-molecular hydrogen bonding in alcohols"},{"identifier":"D","content":"dipole characters of ethers"}],"correct_options":["C"],"explanation":"Alcohol and ether are isomer with each other. So, with same molecular formula we can make ether  as well as alcohol. \n<br><br>For ex, \n<br><br>With molecular formula C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O \n<br><br>(1) <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.133ex\" height=\"0.036ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 0 501 16\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(167,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(334,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>  alcohol will be CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub> OH\n<br><br>(2) <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.133ex\" height=\"0.036ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 0 501 16\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(167,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(334,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ether will be CH<sub>3</sub> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> O <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> CH<sub>3</sub> \n<br><br>In Alcohol there is hydrogen bond and in Ether there is Van der walls force of attraction. \n<br><br>We know that H bond is stronger bond than van der walls force of attraction as the atoms of alcohol are strongly attached with each other by hydrogen bonding so tendency of vaporization of alcohol is less compared to ether.\n<br><br>In alcohol inter-molecular hydrogen bonding look like this -\n<br><br><img class=\"question-image\" src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/hzWiFcmg3sGeMCPeN/GHZPl17wcQPhgE2NvhUdyz9hOQGNq/VIy3cXT94XVCecyegHr1TP/image.png\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2003 Chemistry - Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers Question 184 English Explanation\">  "}]}